  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(1)={0}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{\u2218}$ does not lie inside the required domain, we must find other solutions.\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {360}^{\u2218},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find a solution in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\cdot {360}^{\u2218}={360}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{\u2218}<x\\le {360}^{\u2218}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={360}^{\u2218}$ is the only solution.",
